explain.narrative:
## Core concept
ALL is used with a comparison operator and a subquery. The comparison must be true for every value returned by the subquery. For a non-empty set of non-NULL values, greater than ALL can be rewritten as greater than the maximum value. ALL over an empty set is true; NULL values can produce UNKNOWN.

## Syntax
```sql
-- Dialect support varies; PostgreSQL and MySQL support quantified comparisons.
SELECT columns
FROM outer_table AS o
WHERE o.value > ALL (SELECT value FROM comparison_table);
```

## SQLite sample database
The following examples use a small fictional dataset. Each worked example starts with a repeatable setup script so it can be run independently in the SQL Playground.

## Worked examples
### 1. Find products priced above every target (NOT EXISTS rewrite)
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT p.product_name, p.price FROM products AS p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM price_targets AS t WHERE p.price <= t.target_price) ORDER BY p.price;
```
Expected result: Desk Lamp | 120

With non-NULL values, no target is greater than or equal to 120, so the product is greater than every target.

### 2. Find products cheaper than every target
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT p.product_name, p.price FROM products AS p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM price_targets AS t WHERE p.price >= t.target_price) ORDER BY p.price;
```
Expected result: Pen Set | 15

The NOT EXISTS condition succeeds only when there is no target that the product price is greater than or equal to.

### 3. Use a maximum-value rewrite
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT product_name, price FROM products WHERE price > (SELECT MAX(target_price) FROM price_targets) ORDER BY price;
```
Expected result: Desk Lamp | 120

For this non-empty, non-NULL comparison set, greater than ALL is equivalent to greater than MAX.

### 4. Find products below the maximum target
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT product_name, price FROM products WHERE price < (SELECT MAX(target_price) FROM price_targets) ORDER BY price;
```
Expected result: Pen Set | 15; Notebook | 40; Backpack | 90

This is a comparison with the maximum target, not a less-than ALL test; it demonstrates why the comparison direction matters.

### 5. Return products meeting the strict all-target rule
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS price_targets;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE price_targets (target_price NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',40),(2,'Pen Set',15),(3,'Desk Lamp',120),(4,'Backpack',90);
INSERT INTO price_targets VALUES (20),(50),(100);
SELECT p.product_name FROM products AS p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM price_targets AS t WHERE NOT (p.price > t.target_price)) ORDER BY p.product_id;
```
Expected result: Desk Lamp

NOT EXISTS searches for a counterexample to the required comparison. With the supplied NOT NULL values, it matches the ALL meaning.

## Query behaviour and dialect notes
Quantified comparison syntax (ANY/SOME/ALL) is not supported by SQLite. The runnable SQLite exercises use logically equivalent EXISTS or NOT EXISTS rewrites over the supplied non-NULL values. PostgreSQL and MySQL support quantified comparisons; verify the exact feature and syntax against the target database version. Empty subqueries and NULL values require separate reasoning and are not interchangeable with simple MIN/MAX rewrites.

## Common mistakes
- Assuming SQLite accepts ANY/SOME/ALL quantified syntax.
- Treating ANY as ALL or confusing their truth conditions.
- Ignoring empty-subquery behaviour.
- Applying MIN/MAX rewrites when NULLs or empty sets change the semantics.
- Forgetting that a single counterexample defeats an ALL comparison.

## Best practices
Use explicit aliases and readable predicates. Test edge cases such as missing matches, duplicate values, empty sets and NULLs. Keep the database dialect visible whenever a feature is not portable.

